That's Episode 100, a big milestone.

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:16.180
Thank you for being here.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:21.820
Picture a card game where every choice builds on the last one, and one wrong move can end

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:22.260
your run.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:28.680
Researchers just taught AI agents to master that kind of challenge in Slay the Spire 2.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.920
They replace the usual growing chat log with five separate memory layers that stay organized.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:40.440
Think of it like the difference between scribbling everything on one giant messy notebook page

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:45.540
versus using separate folders for your class notes, homework ideas, and reminders.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:53.360
The AI keeps its prompt size around 5,000 tokens instead of ballooning past 500,000, so it

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:54.060
stays focused.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:59.920
This matters for anyone who plays strategy games or wants AI helpers that can stick with

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.740
a project for more than a few minutes.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:07.320
In school, it could mean an AI that remembers your whole essay plan instead of forgetting

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.280
what you said three messages ago.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.980
For future careers, it points toward assistance that could help with multi-step creative work

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:19.620
like planning a video project or tracking ideas across a long story.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:25.320
If you like card games or rogue likes, this shows AI getting better at the kind of planning

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.520
humans do naturally.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:33.040
You can try the game yourself right now on Steam or mobile versions of Slay the Spire to

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.180
see the challenge these agents are tackling.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:40.780
The researchers approach might soon appear in other AI tools that need to remember context

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:41.560
over time.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.300
You know how when you're playing a long video game session, you might keep a few notes

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:51.300
on paper or in your head about which items you found and what the bosses patterns are.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:56.960
Now picture the AI doing something similar but with five separate notebooks instead of one

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:58.240
giant running list.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:05.080
One notebook holds short-term moves, another tracks long-term goals, a third remembers past

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:06.760
mistakes, and so on.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.500
Each time the AI makes a decision, it only looks at the right notebook instead of rereading

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.660
every single thing it has ever said.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:19.860
This keeps the information it needs right at the front without the whole conversation turning

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.900
into an unreadable wall of text.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:27.700
The result is an AI that can plan several steps ahead in a game without losing track of

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:28.580
earlier details.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.740
It feels less like talking to someone with a terrible memory and more like working with

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:36.000
a teammate who actually keeps good records.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:41.900
That setup is what let these agents finally beat the game where earlier versions got lost

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:42.980
in their own notes.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.120
The layers act like different drawers in a desk.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.440
You pull out only the drawer you need instead of dumping everything on the floor.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.740
Over time, this could help AI handle tasks that stretch across many turns or many days.

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:01.500
The key is that the model never has to carry the entire history at once.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:04.880
It just reaches for the right drawer when it needs it.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:11.560
An economics professor at Brown University noticed his students were scoring 96% on take-home

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:12.100
exams.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:18.500
Then the average crashed to 48% when the same test happened in person with no AI allowed.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:23.480
Many students dropped the class or didn't show up once they realized they couldn't use

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:23.960
the tool.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:29.860
This matters because it shows the gap between using AI to finish work and actually learning

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:30.560
the material.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:35.640
If you're a student, it raises the question of how much AI should help with assignments

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:39.440
versus how much you need to do yourself to build real skills.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:43.360
You can try this experiment in a low stakes way.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.060
Pick a topic you're studying.

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:48.660
Ask chat GPT or Claude to explain it.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:54.260
Then close the chat and explain the same idea out loud to a friend or even to yourself without

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:56.020
looking back at the AI response.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:03.300
Open AI posted a job listing for a product manager focused on experiences for families, caregivers

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:04.640
and older adults.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:09.600
This suggests they're thinking about how the same chatbot could work differently for

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.780
parents helping with homework or grandparents staying in touch.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:18.460
It connects to everyday life because many teens already use chat GPT for school.

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:22.560
Families might soon have shared accounts or kid-friendly modes.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:29.000
The move shows AI companies realizing their tools are moving from solo use into household routines.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:35.120
If you have a chat GPT account, try asking it to explain a topic at different levels.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:40.020
Once like it's talking to a 10-year-old and once like it's talking to a parent.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:44.800
Notice how the same model can shift its style without changing the facts.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:52.860
Open AI's CEO Sam Altman now says he's pretty sure AI is creating more jobs than it removes.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.660
This marks a clear shift from his earlier warnings about mass unemployment.

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:02.900
Studies so far show mixed results rather than clear proof either way.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:07.520
The statement comes directly from recent comments reported by the decoder.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:13.700
This directly affects anyone thinking about careers in tech, creative fields or office work.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:18.500
The overall picture remains uncertain as new data continues to emerge.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:23.660
Watching both the tools and the job data make sense while the evidence develops.

